[36m(TaskRunner pid=8432)[0m Once Max found an electronic calculator from his grandfather Dovlet's chest. He noticed that the numbers were written with seven-segment indicators (https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Seven-segment_display). [Image] 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=8432)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=8432)[0m Max starts to type all the values from a to b. After typing each number Max resets the calculator. Find the total number of segments printed on the calculator.
[36m(TaskRunner pid=8432)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=8432)[0m For example if a = 1 and b = 3 then at first the calculator will print 2 segments, then — 5 segments and at last it will print 5 segments. So the total number of printed segments is 12.
[36m(TaskRunner pid=8432)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=8432)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=8432)[0m -----Input-----
[36m(TaskRunner pid=8432)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=8432)[0m The only line contains two integers a, b (1 ≤ a ≤ b ≤ 10^6) — the first and the last number typed by Max.
[36m(TaskRunner pid=8432)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=8432)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=8432)[0m -----Output-----
[36m(TaskRunner pid=8432)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=8432)[0m Print the only integer a — the total number of printed segments.
[36m(TaskRunner pid=8432)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=8432)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=8432)[0m -----Examples-----...[+73 chars]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=8432)[0m [code] def total_segments(a, b):
[36m(TaskRunner pid=8432)[0m     if a == 1:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=8432)[0m         return 2 + 5 * (b - a)
[36m(TaskRunner pid=8432)[0m     else:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=8432)[0m         return 2 + 5 * (b - 2) + 5 + (b - 2 + a - 3) % 6 * 5
[36m(TaskRunner pid=8432)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=8432)[0m a, b = map(int, input().split())
[36m(TaskRunner pid=8432)[0m print(total_segments(a, b))
